kwbackup (0.0.20) unstable; urgency=medium

  * docs: the shipped defaults file covers every built-in default

    /etc/kwbackup/defaults.yaml is what people open to learn the defaults, so it
    now lists every key of BUILTIN_DEFAULTS: runtime.keep_temp, rclone.binary,
    rclone.env, database.mode, archive.name/include/exclude/mode and security
    were missing.

    The file stays editable: a removed key falls back to the built-in value.
    Retention is a host default for the database only; the archive is not pruned
    if retention is absent, which is now stated in the config and in the docs.

    tests: invariant that examples/defaults.yaml is a superset of
    BUILTIN_DEFAULTS (key paths + mechanical values), 24/24.

 -- Karel Wintersky <karel.wintersky@gmail.com>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:34:23 +0300

kwbackup (0.0.19) unstable; urgency=medium

  * feat: explicit copy mode for database and archive, -m sync mirrors

    Built-in defaults get mode: copy for database and archive. The config accepts
    only 'copy' there: mode: sync is rejected with a pointer to -m sync, so the
    destructive path is reachable only from the command line.

    -m sync now works for all three actions. A staging directory with a hardlink
    to the finished file is uploaded with rclone sync, so the target keeps only
    the newest file; retention is skipped because there is nothing to prune. The
    explanation is logged at debug level, i.e. visible only with --verbose.

    tests: config accepts copy and rejects sync, archive and database mirroring
    (23/23).

 -- Karel Wintersky <karel.wintersky@gmail.com>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:31:46 +0300

kwbackup (0.0.18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * refactor(config): keep the whole validation schema in one dict

    retention and source were added to SECTIONS after _parse_retention, far from
    the rest of the schema. They are entries of the same dictionary, so they now
    live in the literal, and the constant groups carry a one-line explanation of
    what uses them.

